+This page proves asymptotic separation for finite IID sources sent through finite discrete memoryless channels. A code maps a source block to a channel input block and decodes the output block; the channel-use ratio has a fixed limit $n_k/k\to\rho\in(0,\infty)$. The lossless threshold compares source entropy in nats with channel capacity in nats per use. The lossy threshold compares the finite information rate-distortion value in bits—converted to nats—with the same channel budget. Distortion is finite and bounded, and the rate-distortion budget is feasible and attained. The achievability arguments use maximal-error channel codes because a source-code index need not be uniform; the converses use information bounds and do not require channel-code constructions. The results concern asymptotic block criteria and make no finite-block, zero-delay, or universal matching claim.
 
